  "facts": [
    "Redheugh Bridge first opened to pedestrians on 1 May 1871, featuring a 743‑foot truss supported by three iron piers, each resting on a 12‑foot caisson sunk 60 feet below high water.",
    "The 1901 replacement, built by Sir William Arrol & Co., ingeniously slid its new superstructure onto the old piers using hydraulic jacks, a rare construction trick of the era.",
    "The current 1983 concrete span, 26 m above the Tyne, can bear 400‑tonne loads and was designed to resist a 10‑thousand‑tonne vessel traveling five knots, an unlikely but impressive safety margin."
  ],
